<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    The play wedge: Ripcord's action language, and on a console card it IS the primary action rather than an
    icon sitting inside a button.

    Two shapes:

      the zone   a slanted band at the trailing edge, leaning forward like an italic. It is what makes "the
                 whole card is one focus stop" visually true - a card does not need a Connect button when its
                 trailing edge already says it launches something.
      the mark   the play triangle from brand/ripcord-mark.svg, "M29 17 L53 32 L29 47 Z" with a round join.
                 brand/*.svg is the source of truth; keep this in step with it rather than re-drawing it.

    WHY THE GEOMETRY IS BUILT IN CODE AND NOT A VIEWBOX.

    The first version wrapped a fixed 92x176 canvas in a Uniform Viewbox, which is wrong in two ways at once
    and only looks right at exactly one size. Uniform letterboxes: asked for 184 wide beside a 150-tall hero
    card it would scale to the height and leave the zone 78 wide, so the wedge simply would not reach the
    card's edge. Stretch="Fill" fixes the size and breaks the angle instead - the slant would be near 9
    degrees on a grid card and near 20 on the hero, which is the one thing about this shape that has to stay
    constant, because the diagonal is the app's signature and a signature that changes slope is not one.

    So the slant is held as a RATIO of the height (28/176, the mark's own proportion) and the path is rebuilt
    on every size change. The angle is then identical at 92px beside a grid card and at 184px beside the
    hero, which is what docs/design.md means by "it scales with its container, not as a fixed chip".

    ON THE UNREACHABLE CASE, WHICH DEPARTS FROM THE WRITTEN DESIGN - see Muted in the code-behind. In short:
    docs/design.md said an unreachable console has no wedge at all, that was written when "cannot be reached"
    was believed to be knowledge, and it is not. The wedge goes quiet instead of vanishing. The design doc
    records the amendment; if the two ever disagree again, the doc wins and this comment is what to correct.
-->
<UserControl
    x:Class="Ripcord_App.Controls.PlayWedge"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    mc:Ignorable="d"
    SizeChanged="OnSizeChanged">

    <!--
        Decorative to assistive technology. The card hosting this composes its own AutomationName from the
        console's name, its state and what activating it will do, so announcing the wedge as well would add a
        second voice saying "play" after the sentence that already said it.
    -->
    <Grid AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw">

        <!--
            ALL THREE PATHS LIVE IN A CANVAS, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN TIDY.

            Their geometry is rebuilt in OnSizeChanged. In a Grid that is a feedback loop: assigning Data
            changes the Path's desired size, which invalidates layout, which raises SizeChanged again. The
            plane alone survived it by accident - its quadrilateral fills the container exactly, so its
            desired size never changed. The rim is a single diagonal, its bounds are smaller, and adding it
            turned the whole window black with no exception thrown and nothing in the crash log.

            A Canvas does not size to its children, so the loop cannot start. Add shapes here, not to the Grid.
            The Viewbox stays a Grid child because it needs centring, and it is sized from code.
        -->
        <Canvas>

            <!--
                The plane. Its brush comes from the CALLER, for the same reason FamilyMark takes its accent
                from the caller rather than defaulting: a UserControl that names app-level resources in its
                own markup is a lookup waiting to fail, and in XAML that failure is silent.
            -->
            <Path x:Name="Zone" Fill="{x:Bind FacetFill, Mode=OneWay}" />

            <!--
                The bleed: the accent falling off the rim into the plane, built in code from AccentColor.

                This is what stops the rim reading as a drawn edge rather than a lit one, and it is the
                answer to "the wedge is too quiet" that does NOT reintroduce the slab - it is a gradient to
                transparent, so there is no field of colour anywhere, only a falloff. Suppressed entirely
                when Muted, and it carries no information a colour-blind or high-contrast user could miss,
                because the rim and the mark both say the same thing without it.
            -->
            <Path x:Name="Bleed" />

            <!--
                The rim: the leading diagonal alone, in the family's accent. A LINE, NOT A FIELD - this is
                the wedge's whole colour budget and Ripcord.Card.xaml has the argument for why that matters.
                Thickness is a literal rather than a token, for the resource-lookup reason above.
            -->
            <Path
                x:Name="Rim"
                Stroke="{x:Bind RimBrush, Mode=OneWay}"
                StrokeThickness="3" />
        </Canvas>

        <!--
            The mark, centred in the zone. Its own Viewbox so the triangle keeps the brand proportions
            whatever the zone is doing, and its round join is the mark's own - stroke-linejoin="round" in the
            SVG - which is what stops it reading as a sharp arrowhead.
        -->
        <Viewbox
            x:Name="MarkBox"
            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
            VerticalAlignment="Center"
            Stretch="Uniform">
            <Canvas Width="30" Height="36">
                <Path
                    Data="M 5,3 L 27,18 L 5,33 Z"
                    Fill="{x:Bind MarkFill, Mode=OneWay}"
                    Stroke="{x:Bind MarkFill, Mode=OneWay}"
                    StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                    StrokeThickness="6" />
            </Canvas>
        </Viewbox>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
